  <title>Calibrate with an IT8 target and make film profiles</title>
  <section id="it8"><title>Calibrate the scanner with an IT8 target</title>
    <p>An IT8 target is a slide with a set of colour patches that have been measured. Scanning it lets the application learn how your scanner sees colour, and make a scanner profile. This needs ArgyllCMS (<app>scanin</app> and <app>colprof</app>) to be installed.</p>
    <p>You need the measured values for your target's batch. They are not part of the application, because they belong to the maker of the target and differ from batch to batch: a profile made with another batch's values can be several times less accurate. The batch code is printed on the slide, small; it shows up in a scan if you cannot read it on the film. For a target from <link href="https://gwbcolourcard.uk/it8.html">GWB Colour Card</link>, press <gui>Get Data…</gui> in the dialog and download the ZIP of IT8 files. It holds the values for every batch, and you do not need to unpack it. Targets from other makers come with their own file, which you can choose instead.</p>
    <steps>
      <item><p>Choose <gui>Calibrate with IT8 Target…</gui> in the main menu.</p></item>
      <item><p>Put the target slide in the scanner and press <gui>Scan Now</gui>. The whole target is previewed and scanned, and the dialog opens again with the scan and a picture of it. No corrections are needed; the application keeps the raw scan. If you already have a scan, press <gui>Choose File…</gui> instead.</p></item>
      <item><p>Choose the data. A ZIP in your Downloads folder is found for you. Find the batch code in the picture, turning it if it is upside down, and type it into <gui>Batch Code Printed on the Slide</gui>. The field turns red if the code is not in the ZIP. <gui>Most Likely</gui> fills it in with the batch whose grey scale best matches the scan; check it against the printed code.</p></item>
      <item><p>The standard IT8 chart layout is offered and suits most slides. Give the profile a name, or keep the one made from the batch, and press <gui>Build Profile</gui>.</p></item>
      <item><p>When it is done the dialog says how well the profile fits the target, and the profile is used for the frame that is open. It is listed under <gui>Scanner Profile</gui> in the <gui>Colour</gui> group; choose it there for other slides, or make it the default in <guiseq><gui>Preferences</gui><gui>Scanner Profile</gui></guiseq>.</p></item>
    </steps>
    <note style="tip"><p>If the patches cannot be found, check that the whole target is in the scan and that the data file is for your target. A poor fit usually means the data file is for another batch.</p></note>
    <p>The profile is relative to the target's clear film, so a slide comes out white-balanced to its own clear film. Slides are no longer stretched channel by channel once a profile is chosen: they have the colours the profile gives. Negatives ignore the scanner profile; their colour comes from the film profile.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="film"><title>Make a film profile</title>
    <p>Scan unexposed film base together with a grey step wedge exposed on the same film. Open the scan, set the film base with the <gui>Base</gui> tool, and crop the frame around the wedge so that it fills the frame end to end. Then choose <gui>Create Film Profile…</gui> in the main menu, enter the maker, the film's name and speed and the number of steps of the wedge, and press <gui>Create</gui>.</p>
    <p>The profile appears in the list of film profiles. <gui>Import</gui>, <gui>Export</gui> and <gui>Delete</gui> for your own profiles are in the main menu and in <guiseq><gui>Preferences</gui><gui>Film Profiles</gui></guiseq>.</p>
  </section>
